WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.867
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.867 --> 00:00:09.280
Think for a moment about a hot dog.

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:11.510
What comes to mind?

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:17.710
Let me be the first to tell you that
I think the concept of a hot dog is

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:19.610
absolutely revolting.

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:27.408
Pink rubbery mystery meat in a long
tube-like shape that makes you wonder,

00:00:27.408 --> 00:00:32.687
is it really food that most of
us drench in ketchup, mustard,

00:00:32.687 --> 00:00:37.980
sometimes relish to mask
the strange salt-laden flavor?

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:45.690
But believe it or not, I love hot dogs.

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:48.902
Eating a hot dog pulls at my heartstrings.

00:00:48.902 --> 00:00:54.110
To eat a hot dog is to hang out
with my family at a baseball game.

00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:57.056
It's a barbecue with
friends in the backyard.

00:00:57.056 --> 00:01:02.360
And here in the US, it's celebrating
American independence on the 4th of July.

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:06.838
When I think of a hot dog,
I think of family,

00:01:06.838 --> 00:01:09.420
I think of friends, I think of fun.

00:01:11.115 --> 00:01:15.815
My name is Adrian Kerester,
and I love meat.

00:01:15.815 --> 00:01:18.405
But I also care deeply about our planet,
and

00:01:18.405 --> 00:01:22.615
I'm concerned about the meat
industry's impact on our environment.

00:01:23.965 --> 00:01:28.924
After all, the meat industry is
the leading cause of deforestation,

00:01:28.924 --> 00:01:35.010
of water contamination, of air pollution,
and of soil biodiversity loss.

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:39.934
It is also responsible for more than
one-fifth of global greenhouse gas

00:01:39.934 --> 00:01:44.384
emissions, which is more than
all of transportation combined.

00:01:44.384 --> 00:01:50.543
Because of this, I have tried to
cut me out of my diet not once,

00:01:50.543 --> 00:01:55.168
but five times, and
I have failed every time.

00:01:55.168 --> 00:01:59.399
I've heard countless
stories of individuals who,

00:01:59.399 --> 00:02:04.401
after watching one of the many
harrowing documentaries about

00:02:04.401 --> 00:02:09.402
the meat industry,
have cut meat entirely out of their diet,

00:02:09.402 --> 00:02:14.711
exclaiming triumphantly, and
then I never ate meat ever again.

00:02:15.972 --> 00:02:19.158
Was something wrong with me?

00:02:19.158 --> 00:02:24.855
I turn to you, my Stanford classmates,
for some help answering this question.

00:02:24.855 --> 00:02:30.150
Over 50% of you, half of this room,
have tried to cut meat out of

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:35.273
your diet before, and
only a fraction of you have stuck with it.

00:02:35.273 --> 00:02:39.510
I'm not here today to tell you
to cut meat out of your diet.

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:43.300
It's not effective, and
people don't like being told what to do.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:49.398
I'm here today to give you
the meat-lover's guide to environmentally

00:02:49.398 --> 00:02:53.200
conscious eating,
which I will do in two ways.

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:59.620
First, by diagnosing our love for meat,
why is it that we eat meat so much?

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:04.822
And second,
by inspiring individual action,

00:03:04.822 --> 00:03:09.515
sharing with you some tips of
ways that we can collectively

00:03:09.515 --> 00:03:14.592
reduce our meat consumption and
cut our carbon footprint, and

00:03:14.592 --> 00:03:19.890
convincing you that your individual
action does in fact matter.

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:24.290
All right,
let's diagnose our love for meat.

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:31.337
Like a typical consultant, I sent out
a survey, and I crunched the numbers.

00:03:31.337 --> 00:03:35.520
Here are the top three reasons
why we love and eat meat.

00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:41.090
Reason number one, it's the default.

00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:47.620
Over 80% of you shared with me that you
eat meat because it's the default option.

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:52.071
It's easy, it's available, it's what your
friends serve when they host you for

00:03:52.071 --> 00:03:53.060
dinner.

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:55.730
It's embedded in your holiday traditions.

00:03:55.730 --> 00:03:59.840
It's featured in the entrees at
the restaurants you frequent.

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:01.090
The list goes on.

00:04:02.630 --> 00:04:08.586
For me, meat is central to my notion
of what makes a complete meal.

00:04:08.586 --> 00:04:13.085
Growing up,
family dinner was a daily sacred ritual.

00:04:13.085 --> 00:04:17.607
Every evening around 7:30 PM,
my family of four would

00:04:17.607 --> 00:04:22.234
gather around the table to share
a meal and debrief the day.

00:04:22.234 --> 00:04:29.235
My memories of these dinners are deeply
defined by my dad's dinner music,

00:04:29.235 --> 00:04:33.690
dim lighting, candles, good conversation.

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:38.591
And my mom's absolutely
delicious homemade meals that

00:04:38.591 --> 00:04:43.804
were well-rounded and
often followed a simple equation,

00:04:43.804 --> 00:04:48.716
a meat-based meal with two sides,
starch and greens.

00:04:48.716 --> 00:04:52.830
Today, family dinners
are typically vegetarian, and

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:55.992
my notion of what makes
a meal has evolved.

00:04:55.992 --> 00:05:01.711
But I find that I have to work extremely
hard to fight this default bias.

00:05:01.711 --> 00:05:06.375
Until we position plant-based
options as the default, and

00:05:06.375 --> 00:05:09.759
reposition meat as a secondary, or a side,

00:05:09.759 --> 00:05:14.430
or even a specialty option,
meat will always be the go-to.

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:20.601
Reason number two,
it's all about the protein.

00:05:20.601 --> 00:05:24.570
Over 70% of you shared with
me that you eat meat because

00:05:24.570 --> 00:05:28.300
it's a high protein option,
and you're not wrong.

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:34.950
However, we don't need nearly
as much protein as we think.

00:05:34.950 --> 00:05:40.512
In fact, the average American eats
over 100 grams of protein a day,

00:05:40.512 --> 00:05:44.420
which is roughly double
the recommended amount.

00:05:46.070 --> 00:05:48.858
This fact doesn't shock me.

00:05:48.858 --> 00:05:52.746
After all, the diet industry
pours millions of dollars

00:05:52.746 --> 00:05:57.640
into convincing us that protein is
the beacon of health and wellness.

00:05:57.640 --> 00:06:01.611
But it did make me wonder
what foods could I eat to

00:06:01.611 --> 00:06:06.392
get my recommended 50 grams
of protein a day sans meat?

00:06:06.392 --> 00:06:09.540
It's actually really quite easy.

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:14.082
For example,
instead of eating two servings of chicken,

00:06:14.082 --> 00:06:18.995
one at lunch and one at dinner,
we could eat two eggs at lunch and

00:06:18.995 --> 00:06:24.360
spinach feta chickpea salad
with some almonds at dinner.

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:29.615
We don't need to rely on meat
as our main source of protein.

00:06:31.326 --> 00:06:34.280
And finally, reason number three.

00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:37.580
We still don't really
love the alternatives.

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:41.780
Over 50% of you shared
with me that you eat meat,

00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:47.934
not because you love it, but because
the alternatives are too expensive,

00:06:47.934 --> 00:06:52.930
too carb heavy, too greasy,
and sometimes just too bland.

00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:56.117
So what can we do?

00:06:57.604 --> 00:07:02.862
At the beginning of this presentation,
I shared with you that I'd provide

00:07:02.862 --> 00:07:07.545
the meat-lover's guide to
environmentally conscious eating.

00:07:07.545 --> 00:07:11.790
If you love meat, but you want to
reduce your carbon footprint and

00:07:11.790 --> 00:07:14.390
help save our planet, what can you do?

00:07:14.390 --> 00:07:16.259
Where can you start?

00:07:16.259 --> 00:07:19.311
And does it even matter?

00:07:19.311 --> 00:07:23.502
While there is no
one-size-fits-all option,

00:07:23.502 --> 00:07:28.388
I want to challenge each of you
to do three things this week,

00:07:28.388 --> 00:07:33.094
better meat, in smaller portions,
less frequently.

00:07:33.094 --> 00:07:34.881
Why better meat?

00:07:34.881 --> 00:07:39.003
It turns out that not all
meat is created equal.

00:07:39.003 --> 00:07:43.943
Beef and lamb are significantly more
harmful to the environment than chicken or

00:07:43.943 --> 00:07:44.540
turkey.

00:07:45.640 --> 00:07:51.844
If you do purchase red meat, look for
options that are certified organic, or

00:07:51.844 --> 00:07:58.622
pasture raised to support meat production
systems that don't use environmentally

00:07:58.622 --> 00:08:04.937
harmful pesticides that contaminate
our water, or ruin soil biodiversity.

00:08:04.937 --> 00:08:10.563
Smaller portions, I challenge all of
you to rethink your notion of what

00:08:10.563 --> 00:08:15.931
makes a meal, and try meat as a side or
a topping, instead of a main.

00:08:15.931 --> 00:08:22.738
The USDA recommends a serving size of
just three ounces of meat per meal.

00:08:22.738 --> 00:08:27.531
Which, to put in context, is about
a third of the size of the chicken

00:08:27.531 --> 00:08:31.750
breast that you see typically
at Trader Joe's or Safeway.

00:08:33.230 --> 00:08:38.231
If you're out to eat, try splitting
a meat-based entree with a friend,

00:08:38.231 --> 00:08:41.470
in addition to a vegetarian,
or a fish entree.

00:08:43.550 --> 00:08:47.380
Finally, eat meat less frequently.

00:08:47.380 --> 00:08:50.477
The best thing you can do for
the environment and for

00:08:50.477 --> 00:08:55.392
your long-term health is to reduce the
number of meals you eat containing meat.

00:08:55.392 --> 00:09:02.870
I challenge all of you to pick just one
day each week to dedicate to be meatless.

00:09:02.870 --> 00:09:08.210
Meatless Mondays have grown in popularity
across the country, and around the world.

00:09:09.230 --> 00:09:14.070
As someone who loves the social and
cultural aspects of eating meat,

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:18.922
I actually don't prescribe to
meatless Mondays on a regular basis.

00:09:18.922 --> 00:09:21.650
But I don't cook meat at home anymore.

00:09:22.970 --> 00:09:27.863
This shift has allowed me to drastically
reduce my meat consumption from

00:09:27.863 --> 00:09:31.240
nearly daily,
to now only a fraction of my meals.

00:09:33.030 --> 00:09:38.670
If you take just one thing away from
this presentation, let it be this.

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:45.897
If everyone in the US were to not
eat meat for just one day each week,

00:09:45.897 --> 00:09:51.020
it would be the same as not
driving 91 billion miles.

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:57.496
Which, if you do the math,
is basically the same as not driving from

00:09:57.496 --> 00:10:02.340
San Francisco all the way to New York and
back 15 million times.

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:09.146
Small changes at scale really
do add up to make a difference.

00:10:09.146 --> 00:10:12.375
So I'll leave you with this.

00:10:12.375 --> 00:10:17.138
Better meat, in smaller portions,
less frequently.

00:10:17.138 --> 00:10:22.568
This is something we can do as
individuals with our families and

00:10:22.568 --> 00:10:25.646
role model for our organizations.

00:10:25.646 --> 00:10:31.531
Together with these small changes,
we really can make a big difference.

00:10:31.531 --> 00:10:32.835
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:10:32.835 --> 00:10:37.178
[MUSIC]

